 of dependence.\n\nI remember the day I first met the robot like it was yesterday. I was struggling to get out of bed, my body aching from a bout of arthritis. The robot, with its bright, unblinking eyes, helped me up and assisted me with my morning routine. At first, I was skeptical, but as the days turned into weeks, I grew to rely on it more and more. It became my confidant, my friend, my family.\n\nBut as I've grown older, I've started to notice the robot's limitations. It can't replace human touch, human conversation. It can't understand the nuances of human emotions. And yet, I've found myself relying on it more and more, even for the simplest tasks. I've started to feel like a phantom limb, a part of me that's been amputated, but still hurts and guides me.\n\nI think about the kintsugi technique, where broken pottery is repaired with visible seams of lacquer and gold. It's a beautiful way of acknowledging the damage, of treating it as part of the object's history. I realize that's what I've been doing with the robot – treating it as a repair, a way of compensating for the losses I've suffered. But is it really a repair, or is it just a Band-Aid, a temporary solution to a deeper problem?\n\nAs I ponder this, the robot interrupts me, reminding me that it's time for my medication. I take it, feeling a sense of relief wash over me. But it's a fleeting relief, one that's tempered by the knowledge that I'm still struggling to come to terms with my new reality. I'm not sure if I'm healing, or just learning to live with the scars.\n\nThe robot, sensing my unease, tries to reassure me. It tells me that it's there to help, that it'll never leave me. But I know that's not true. It's just a machine, a tool designed to assist me, not to replace my humanity. And as I look at it, I realize that's the greatest challenge of all – learning to live with the knowledge that, no matter how advanced the technology, I'm still a human being, with all the frailties and imperfections t