ant narratives of their respective contexts. Catch-22 uses satire and absurdity to critique the military's rules and the absurdity of war, while White Noise uses metafiction and pastiche to critique consumer culture and the fragmentation of identity.\n\n**Conclusion and Call to Action**\n\nIn conclusion, Catch-22 and White Noise are two novels that masterfully employ deconstructionist elements to critique the very fabric of reality. As we reflect on these works, we are forced to confront the absurdity of war and the fragmentation of identity. We are left with more questions than answers, and it is up to us to continue the conversation.\n\n**Discussion Questions and Further Reading**\n\n* What are the implications of Catch-22's use of satire and absurdity in critiquing military bureaucracy?\n* How does White Noise's use of metafiction and pastiche reflect the fragmentation of identity in consumer culture?\n* What are the consequences of embracing a postmodern worldview, one that rejects grand narratives and instead celebrates fragmentation and ambiguity?\n\nFor further reading, we recommend the works of Jean-François Lyotard, Jacques Derrida, and Fredric Jameson, who have all written extensively on the topic of postmodern art and its reflection in literary works.\n\n**Reference Material**\n\n* Catch-22 by Joseph Heller\n* White Noise by Don DeLillo\n* Original text excerpts from both novels\n* Literary and artistic background knowledge on postmodern art and its characteristics", "self_check_notes": ["Candidate was repaired from malformed JSON emitted by the model."]}, "candidate_retry_note": {"attempts": 1, "validation_errors": []}, "judgment": {"scores_by_criterion": [{"name": "Script_Structure_Adherence", "score_1_to_10": 8, "evidence": "The script follows a clear outline structure with sections for postmodern art characteristics, comparative analysis of Catch-22 and White Noise, deconstructionist elements, conclusion, and interactive elements.", "main_issue": "The