eller's Catch-22 and Don DeLillo's White Noise. Through a theoretical framework, we will examine the ways in which these authors use satire, absurdity, and fragmentation to critique modern society.\n\n**Overview of Postmodern Art Characteristics**\n\nPostmodern art is characterized by its rejection of grand narratives and its emphasis on fragmentation and chaos. This is reflected in the works of artists such as Marcel Duchamp and Robert Rauschenberg, who challenged traditional notions of art and beauty. In literature, postmodern authors such as Thomas Pynchon and Don DeLillo use similar techniques to subvert traditional narrative structures and challenge readers to question their assumptions.\n\n**Literary Work Case Analysis: Catch-22**\n\nIn Catch-22, Joseph Heller uses satire to critique the military bureaucracy and the absurdity of war. The novel's protagonist, Yossarian, is trapped in a never-ending cycle of missions and paperwork, highlighting the dehumanizing effects of modern warfare. Through Yossarian's character development, Heller shows how the individual is fragmented and reduced to a mere cog in a machine.\n\n*   *Original Text Excerpt*: \"There was only one catch, and that was Catch-22. The rule specified that concern for one's safety in the face of real and immediate danger was the process of a rational mind. Orr was crazy and could be grounded. All he had to do was ask. But as soon as he asked, he would no longer be crazy and would have to fly more missions. If he flew them, he was crazy and didn't have to; if he didn't want to, he was sane and had to.\"\n\n**Literary Work Case Analysis: White Noise**\n\nIn White Noise, Don DeLillo uses metafiction and pastiche to critique consumer culture and the fragmentation of modern society. The novel's protagonist, Jack Gladney, is a professor of Hitler studies who is struggling to make sense of the world around him. Through Jack's character development, DeLillo shows how the individual is reduced to a mere consumer, trapped in a never-ending cycle of consumption and waste.\n\n*   *Original Text Excerpt*: \"We begin in chaos, in babble. When we come into the world with such flourish, we try to devise a form, a scheme. There's dignity in that. Your whole life is a plot, a scheme, a diagram. It's a failed scheme but that's not the point. The point is the attempt to plot, to have a scheme. To give life a shape and a frame, that's the idea. Even after death, especially after death, the search continues. The funeral is a formal attempt to complete the scheme. Think of a state funeral, Jack. How precise, detailed, orderly, systematic. The nation holds its breath. A great and powerful government is forced into a ceremony that reveals the last traces of chaos. If it goes well, if they execute it properly, then some natural law has been honored. The nation is reassured, the dead man's life has been rescinded to God, life itself is strengthened and reaffirmed.\"\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nIn conclusion, the deconstructionist elements in Catch-22 and White Noise are a reflection of the postmodern art movement's emphasis on fragmentation and chaos. Through satire, absurdity, and fragmentation, these authors challenge readers to question their assumptions and think critically about the world around them. As we reflect on these works, we are reminded of the importance of acknowledging and embracing the complexities of life, rather than trying to impose order and control.\n\n**Call for Audience Interaction**\n\n*   *Discussion Questions*:\n    *   How do the authors use satire and absurdity to critique modern society?\n    *   What role does fragmentation play in the deconstructionist elements of Catch-22 and White Noise?\n    *   How do the authors use metafiction and pastiche to challenge traditional narrative structures?\n*   *Further Reading Recommendations*:\n    *   Thomas Pynchon's Gravity's Rainbow\n    *   Don DeLillo's Underworld\n    *   Jacques Derrida's Of Grammatology", "self_che