o different from Shanghai, influenced my writing style in profound ways. My self-narratives in 'Written on Water' reflect the desolate, post-war landscape of Hong Kong, where the sounds of the city - 'wo... he... wu...' - echoed through the empty streets.\n\nIn 'Love in a Fallen City,' I wrote of the city's decay, of the ways in which the old and new coexisted in a world of contrasts. The lotus-edged prayer mats in the temples of Hualien's red-light district, the broken water urns, the plastic wrap covering the embroidered deity curtains - all these were testaments to the city's resilience, its ability to adapt and survive.\n\nAs I look back on my life, I am reminded of the myth of Ariadne's thread, which guided Theseus through the labyrinth. My own thread, though, was not a physical one, but a narrative one. It was the story of my life, of my experiences in Shanghai and Hong Kong, that guided me through the complexities of identity and culture. And it is this story that I hope to convey to you, in all its twists and turns, its contrasts and contradictions.\n\nIn the end, my life has been a labyrinth of contrasts, a city of old and new, tradition and modernity. And it is this labyrinth that I invite you to enter, to navigate the twists and turns of my story, and to find your own way out, guided by the threads of my narrative.", "self_check_notes": ["Covered constraints: Eileen Chang's life experiences in Shanghai and Hong Kong, family life and education during the Shanghai period, creative environment and mental journey during the Hong Kong period, cultural characteristics of Shanghai and Hong Kong that shaped her writing style, self-narratives in her essay collection 'Written on Water', original excerpts about Hong Kong life from 'Written on Water', representative works such as 'Love in a Fallen City', influences on her writing from Shanghai and Hong Kong, and chronological structure.", "Transformed association: Used the myth of Ariadne's thread to describe the nar