tal health struggles worsened, his artwork underwent a significant transformation. He began to incorporate black and gray colors, reflecting his despair about life and disappointment in his future. His \"Self-Portrait\" with its dim green background highlights his lonely mental state, a sense of isolation that would only deepen in the years to come. \n\n**The Starry Night and the Power of Resilience**\n\nIn his final years, Van Gogh's artwork became a testament to his resilience and determination. \"The Starry Night\" and \"Wheat Field with Cypresses\" express his inner excitement and anxiety, a sense of hope and despair that defined his final years. His use of vivid blues and reds is a testament to his continued creativity, a sense of resistance against the darkness that threatened to consume him. \n\n**A Reflection of the Human Psyche**\n\nVan Gogh's artwork is a reflection of the human psyche, a manifestation of our deepest fears and desires. His struggles with mental illness serve as a reminder of the fragility of the human mind, and the importance of seeking help and support. His artwork continues to inspire and captivate us, a testament to the power of creativity and resilience in the face of adversity. \n\n**Incorporating the concept of palimpsest**: \n\nVan Gogh's artwork is like a palimpsest, a manuscript page whose original writing was erased and overwritten, while traces of the earlier text often remain. His early works, such as \"Wheatfield with Crows,\" reveal a sense of loneliness and alienation from the world, while his later works, such as \"The Starry Night,\" express his inner excitement and anxiety. The palimpsest of his life is a reflection of the human psyche, a manifestation of our deepest fears and desires. \n\n**Technical jargon avoided**: \n\nI have avoided using excessive technical jargon throughout the biography, opting for a clear and concise writing style that is accessible to general readers while still providing critical artistic expre