ung calligrapher protagonist", "traditional vs modern artistic style", "family event as plot turning point", "calligraphy techniques", "emotional tension", "weight of culture", "narrative drama", "delicate and literary writing style"], "must_avoid": ["dragging strokes", "dry strokes", "uneven thickness", "too many \"eyes\" in a single character"], "form_or_structure": ["novel manuscript", "plot with turning point", "narrative with emotional tension", "calligraphy techniques descriptions"], "style_or_tone": ["delicate", "literary", "authentic", "reflective"], "open_questions_or_ambiguities": ["How to balance traditional and modern artistic styles?", "How to effectively depict the weight of culture?", "How to maintain narrative drama while showcasing calligraphy techniques?"], "likely_failure_modes": ["Lack of authenticity in calligraphy techniques", "Insufficient character development", "Inconsistent tone or style", "Unclear plot turning point"]}, "branch_expansion_summary": {"branch_count": 3, "all_branch_ids": ["A", "B", "C"]}, "status": "drafted", "candidate": {"branch_id": "B", "candidate_answer": "The Brush of Tradition\n\nIn the small town where I grew up, calligraphy was not just a craft, but a family legacy. My father's studio was a revered institution, where generations of artists had honed their skills and perfected the ancient art of brushwork. As a young calligrapher, I had always been fascinated by the intricate strokes and delicate flourishes that seemed to dance across the page. But as I grew older, I began to feel suffocated by the weight of tradition. My father's expectations were clear: I was to follow in his footsteps, to master the traditional styles and techniques that had been passed down through our family for generations.\n\nBut I had other ideas. I wanted to break free from the shackles of convention and forge my own path. I began to experiment with modern styles and techniques, incorporating bold colors and abstract shapes into my work. My father was appalled, seeing my innovations as a betrayal of our family's heritage. The tension between us grew, until it seemed as though our very relationship was at stake.\n\nIt was at this point that our annual calligraphy exhibition approached, a event that had been a highlight of our town's cultural calendar for years. I knew that this would be my chance to showcase my work and prove to my father that I was more than just a traditionalist. But as I set up my display, I couldn't shake the feeling that I was walking a tightrope, balancing the weight of tradition and the thrill of innovation.\n\nThe exhibition was a resounding success, with my modern pieces stealing the show. But as I looked out at the crowd, I saw my father's face, his expression a mix of pride and disappointment. I knew then that I had made the right decision, that my art was not just a reflection of our family's legacy, but a reflection of my own unique voice.\n\nAs I worked on my pieces, I had drawn inspiration from the concept of Kintsugi, the Japanese art of repairing broken pottery with visible seams of lacquer and gold. I saw parallels between the beauty of brokenness and the beauty of imperfection in art. My father, on the other hand, was more concerned with the erasure of mistakes, with covering up the flaws and imperfections that made our family's calligraphy unique. I realized that our differing views on art were not just about technique, but about the very nature of identity and tradition.\n\nIn the end, I came to understand that my art was not just a reflection of our family's legacy, but a reflection of my own journey, my own struggles to find my place within the world of calligraphy. And as I looked at my father, I saw that he too was on a journey, one of acceptance and understanding. Our family's calligraphy studio was not just a place of tradition, but a place of growth, of evolution, of the beautiful imperfections that make us who we are.\n\nThe calligraphy techniques I used in my pieces were inspired by the principles of traditional Chinese calligraphy, where the emphasis is on the process, not just the product. I wanted to capture the essence of this process, the fluid movement of the brush, the flow of ink, the dance of the strokes. I experimented with different techniques, such as cursive script, horizontal strokes, and ink quality, to create a sense of depth and texture. My goal was not just to create beautiful art, but to evoke a sense of emotion, to convey the weight of tradition and the thrill of innovation.\n\nAs I worked, I was aware of the palimpsest-like quality of our family's history, the layers of tradition and innovation that had been written over and over again. I saw parallels between this process and the way our family's calligraphy had been passed down through the generations, each artist building on the work of the previous one. I wanted to capture this sense of continuity and evolution in my art, to show that our family's legacy was not just a static entity, but a living, breathing thing that was constantly changing and adapting.\n\nIn the end, my art was not just a reflection of our family's legacy, but a reflection of our shared journey, a journey of growth, of evolution, of the beautiful imperfections that make us who we are.", "self_check_notes": ["Covered constraints: novel set in a calligrapher's family, inheritance of art and inner conflict, young calligrapher protagonist, traditional vs modern artistic style, family event as plot turning point, calligraphy techniques, emotional tension, weight of culture, narrative drama. Transformed association: Kintsugi used to reflect the beauty of imperfection in art and identity."]}, "candidate_retry_note": {"attempts": 1, "validation_errors": []}, "judgment": {"branch_id": "B", "scores_by_criterion": [{"name": "Calligraphic_Technical_Authenticity", "score_1_to_10": 7, "evidence": "The candidate describes various calligraphy techniques and principles, such as cursive scrip