ents, climax, and ending", "atmosphere inspired by Japanese mystery novel 'Another'", "minimum word count: 5000 words", "typical scenes and character relationships from real school life", "psychological insights into teenagers", "urban legends/supernatural phenomena", "collections of school horror stories"], "must_avoid": ["excessive violence or gore", "unrealistic or implausible plot twists"], "form_or_structure": ["outline format", "story structure: introduction, development, climax, and conclusion", "horror elements: mysterious creatures, ghosts, bloodstains"], "style_or_tone": ["eerie atmosphere", "suspense and tension", "horror elements"], "open_questions_or_ambiguities": ["how to balance realistic and supernatural elements", "how to create a believable and engaging plot", "how to effectively use the outline format to guide the story"], "likely_failure_modes": ["insufficient character development", "overuse of horror elements", "inconsistent tone and atmosphere"]}, "branch_expansion_summary": {"branch_count": 3, "all_branch_ids": ["A", "B", "C"]}, "status": "drafted", "candidate": {"branch_id": "C", "candidate_answer": "The School of Shadows\n\nAs I stepped into the School of Shadows, I couldn't shake off the feeling that something was off. The building seemed to loom over me, its walls whispering secrets in the wind. I had heard the rumors, of course – who hadn't? – but I never thought much of them. That was until I saw her. A girl in a school uniform, her eyes black as coal, her skin pale as the moon. She was standing in the hallway, her gaze fixed on me, and I felt a chill run down my spine.\n\nI tried to brush it off as mere imagination, but the feeling lingered. And then, I started to notice the little things. The way the staircases seemed to shift and change, like they were alive. The way the shadows seemed to move of their own accord. It was as if the school was trying to tell me something, but I couldn't quite decipher the message.\n\nI began to investigate, talking to my classmates, scouring the school's history books. And that's when I stumbled upon it – the story of a girl who had died in the school, her spirit trapped between worlds. They said she was a victim of bullying, that her classmates had driven her to madness and despair. But I knew that wasn't the whole story. There was something more, something that lay just beyond the surface.\n\nI started to experience strange and vivid dreams, full of images and symbols that seemed to hold hidden meanings. I would see myself walking through the school's corridors, the walls melting away like wax, revealing a labyrinth of twisted passages and dark chambers. And in the center of it all, the girl in the school uniform, her eyes black as coal, her skin pale as the moon.\n\nI knew then that I had to uncover the truth. I had to follow the threads of the mystery, no matter where they led. And so, I set out on a journey through the School of Shadows, a journey that would take me to the very heart of the school's darkest secrets.\n\nAs I delved deeper, I began to realize that the school was a microcosm of the world outside. The same fears, the same anxieties, the same petty cruelties that plagued the world at large were present here, in this place of supposed learning and growth. And at the center of it all, the girl in the school uniform, her presence a constant reminder of the darkness that lurked within us all.\n\nI encountered many people on my journey, each with their own story, their own secrets. There was Emma, the popular girl who seemed to have it all, but was hiding a deep sense of insecurity. There was Jake, the star athlete who was haunted by the ghost of his former teammate. And there was Mrs. Johnson, the kind-hearted teacher who seemed to know more than she let on.\n\nAs I navigated the twists and turns of the school's corridors, I began to realize that the line between reality and the supernatural was thin indeed. The school's ghosts and monsters were not 