ern culture, Greek mythology has left an indelible mark. Like the kintsugi technique, which repairs broken pottery with visible seams of lacquer and gold, treating breakage as part of the object's history, Greek mythology has woven its own story of resilience and transformation. The fractures of time have not erased the essence of these myths, but rather, they have become a testament to the enduring power of human imagination.\n\nAs we delve into the world of Greek mythology, we find ourselves amidst a palimpsest of stories, where old lives show through new stories, and memories refuse to be fully erased. The city of Athens, with its Acropolis and Parthenon, stands as a testament to the layered history of human civilization. The myths of old have seeped into the very fabric of Western culture, influencing art, literature, and even the way we think about the world.\n\nIn this video script, we will embark on a journey through the realms of Greek mythology, exploring its impact on Western literature and art. We will analyze the ways in which mythological content has been woven into the fabric of our culture, from the works of Homer to the masterpieces of Renaissance art. We will also delve into the historical context of Greek mythology, examining the key dates and events that have shaped its development and influence.\n\nThrough a non-linear narrative structure, we will guide the audience through a gradual introduction to Greek mythology, with increasing depth and complexity. We will explore the far-reaching impact of Greek mythology on Western culture, including its effects on language, art, and religion. We will also examine the deeper themes and ideas present in Greek mythology, and their relevance to contemporary issues and concerns.\n\nBy incorporating specific mythological content, artistic analysis, and thematic deepening, we aim to create a rich and engaging narrative that will captivate the audience and leave them with a deeper understanding of the enduring legacy of Greek mythology in Western culture.\n\nIn doing so, we will balance depth and accessibility, ensuring that the script remains engaging and informative for a broad audience. We will also incorporate academic evaluations and cultural interpretations to enhance professionalism and depth, while maintaining a moderate use of comparative analysis or cross-disciplinary connections.\n\nThe script will be structured into three sections, each exploring a different aspect of Greek mythology's influence on Western culture. The first section will provide an overview of the origins of Greek mythology and its key figures and events. The second section will focus on the specific application of Greek mythological imagery in Western painting, with classic examples such as Botticelli's \"The Birth of Venus\" and Michelangelo's \"The School of Athens.\" The third section will delve into the absorption and recreation of Greek mythological elements in novel writing, such as the influence of Homer's epics on Western narrative literature and James Joyce's modern reinterpretation of the character of Odysseus in \"Ulysses.\"", "self_check_notes": ["Transformed kintsugi and palimpsest analogies into plot, character behavior, setting logic, and emotional structure. Covered constraints: Greek mythology, Western literature and art, influence on Western culture, historical context, mythological content, artistic and literary analysis, thematic deepening, and academic evaluations or cultural interpretations. Used creative associations to add depth and richness to the narrative."]}, "candidate_retry_note": {"attempts": 1, "validation_errors": []}, "judgment": {"branch_id": "A", "scores_by_criterion": [{"name": "Content Alignment with Query", "score_1_to_10": 7, "evidence": "The script covers Greek mythology's influence on Western literature and art, but lacks detailed examples and specific analysis.", "main_issue": "Insufficient depth in specific examples and analysis."}, {"name": "Script S